            <ArticleTitle>Ductile Glass of Polyrotaxane Toughened by Stretch-Induced Intramolecular Phase Separation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>32436-32440</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acsami.7b10845</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A new class of ductile glasses is created from a thermoplastic polyrotaxane. The hard glass, which has a Young's modulus of 1 GPa, shows crazing, necking, and strain hardening with a total elongation of 330%. Stress concentration is prevented through a unique stretch-induced intramolecular phase separation of the cyclic components and the exposed backbone. In situ synchrotron X-ray scattering studies indicate that the backbone polymer chains slip through the cyclic components in the regions where the stress is concentrated.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
